  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $16B on contracts in FY2026 to date. The Department executed 23,478 awards with an average award size of $680K. The Department's obligations fell 25.0% year over year compared with the prior fiscal period. Fiscal-year totals show a decline from $34.3B in FY21 through $21.3B in FY25 down to $16B in FY26. The Department concentrated spending in a few program areas: Computer Systems Design Services accounted for $2.5B, Other Computer Related Services accounted for $1.4B, and Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services accounted for $1.3B. The top vendors in FY2026 to date include GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. at $750M and MANHATTAN TORCON A JOINT VENTURE at $492M. The Department's purchasing picture mixes large IT services obligations with health research, construction, and pharmaceutical manufacturing buys, and spending is currently lower than in recent fiscal years.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed in FY2026 to date for HHS contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $16B in FY2026 to date, down from $21.3B in FY25. The Department's obligations fell 25.0% year over year, and award volume stands at 23,478 with an average award size of $680K. The Department's recent fiscal trend shows declines from $34.3B in FY21 to $16B in FY26."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where did HHS put contract dollars in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Health and Human Services directed the largest NAICS share to Computer Systems Design Services at $2.5B, followed by Other Computer Related Services at $1.4B and Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services at $1.3B. The Department also obligated funds for biotechnology R&D and custom computer programming, each at $1.1B, and for pharmaceutical manufacturing at $550M."
      }
    ]
  },
